course: dictionary
lesson: 16026
title: Vengeance: getting even
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Vengeance means harming someone because they have harmed you or someone you love. It is another word for revenge. «The dragon burned Sam's village, so Sam swore vengeance.» The dragon hurt Sam's village, and now Sam wants to hurt the dragon back. The word swore tells you that Sam made a serious promise to get even.
explain: The phrase «with a vengeance» means very strongly, or much more than usual. «It rained with a vengeance all afternoon.» The rain did not just fall. It poured hard and fierce, much harder than normal. Check the meaning by swapping in "very strongly": it still makes sense.
explain: Stories often use vengeance to show what the plot is about. When a character swears vengeance, the reader knows the story will be about revenge. Many people also think about whether vengeance is a good idea. Many church teachings say that seeking revenge often makes things worse, and the Bible teaches that people should not take revenge themselves. Jesus taught people to love their enemies.
fact: Vengeance means harming someone because they have harmed you or someone you love.
fact: Vengeance is another word for revenge.
fact: «Swear vengeance» means make a serious promise to get revenge.
fact: «With a vengeance» means very strongly, or much more than usual.
fact: Many church teachings say that seeking vengeance often makes things worse, and Jesus taught people to love their enemies.
exercise: «The dragon burned Sam's village, so Sam swore vengeance.» What does vengeance mean here? | Sleeping for a long time | A gift to say thank you | *Harming someone back because they harmed you | Running away to a new town
exercise: «It rained with a vengeance all afternoon.» What does "with a vengeance" mean here? | *Very strongly, much more than usual | Only for a short time | Without any clouds at all | Very gently, like a soft mist
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to vengeance? | happiness | *revenge | sleep | castle
test: «The queen vowed vengeance on the thief who stole her crown.» What does vowed vengeance suggest? | The queen gave the thief a reward | The queen decided to go to sleep | *The queen promised to get revenge on the thief | The queen forgot about the crown
test: Jesus taught people to love their enemies. What does this suggest about vengeance? | Vengeance is always the best choice | Enemies should be given crowns | *Good people should not seek revenge | Vengeance means being very happy
exam: Which sentence uses vengeance correctly? | The sun shone with vengeance on the beach | *After his bike was stolen, Ali sought vengeance on the boy who took it | The baker wanted vengeance and made a cake | The team played with vengeance, scoring many goals
